4. Better Strategy in Competitive Programming
For competitive programming, it is usually better to check the condition before performing the operation.
Example: Avoid division by zero
Instead of:
try {
    int result = a / b;
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Error");
}
Prefer:
if (b != 0) {
    int result = a / b;
    System.out.println(result);
} else {
    System.out.println("Division by zero is not allowed");
}
This is generally simpler and avoids using exceptions for predictable situations.
________________________________________
5. Exception Handling with a Custom Exception
class NegativeNumberException extends Exception {

    NegativeNumberException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}

public class CustomExceptionDemo {

    static void checkNumber(int n) throws NegativeNumberException {

        if (n < 0) {
            throw new NegativeNumberException(
                "Negative numbers are not allowed"
            );
        }

        System.out.println("Valid number: " + n);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {
            checkNumber(-5);
        }
        catch (NegativeNumberException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
Output:
Negative numbers are not allowed
________________________________________
Error-Handling Strategies
Situation	Strategy
Division by zero	Check denominator before division
Invalid array index	Check index range
Invalid input	Validate input
Empty array	Check length == 0
Null object	Check for null
Integer overflow	Use long where required
Invalid mathematical operation	Check conditions
Unexpected runtime error	try-catch
Special application error	Custom exception
Task 3 – Vehicle Rental System
Concept Used
•	Inheritance 
•	Method overriding 
•	Runtime polymorphism 
•	Base class and subclasses 
Java Program
import java.util.*;

class Vehicle {
    String vehicleNumber;
    double rentPerDay;

    Vehicle(String vehicleNumber, double rentPerDay) {
        this.vehicleNumber = vehicleNumber;
        this.rentPerDay = rentPerDay;
    }

    double calculateRent(int days) {
        return rentPerDay * days;
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {

    Car(String vehicleNumber, double rentPerDay) {
        super(vehicleNumber, rentPerDay);
    }

    @Override
    double calculateRent(int days) {
        return rentPerDay * days;
    }
}

class Bike extends Vehicle {

    Bike(String vehicleNumber, double rentPerDay) {
        super(vehicleNumber, rentPerDay);
    }

    @Override
    double calculateRent(int days) {
        return rentPerDay * days * 0.90;
    }
}

class Truck extends Vehicle {

    Truck(String vehicleNumber, double rentPerDay) {
        super(vehicleNumber, rentPerDay);
    }

    @Override
    double calculateRent(int days) {
        return rentPerDay * days * 1.20;
    }
}

public class VehicleRentalSystem {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int N = sc.nextInt();

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {

            int vehicleType = sc.nextInt();
            String vehicleNumber = sc.next();
            double rentPerDay = sc.nextDouble();
            int days = sc.nextInt();

            // Runtime polymorphism
            Vehicle vehicle;

            if (vehicleType == 1) {
                vehicle = new Car(vehicleNumber, rentPerDay);
            }
            else if (vehicleType == 2) {
                vehicle = new Bike(vehicleNumber, rentPerDay);
            }
            else {
                vehicle = new Truck(vehicleNumber, rentPerDay);
            }

            double totalRent = vehicle.calculateRent(days);

            System.out.printf(
                "%s %.2f%n",
                vehicleNumber,
                totalRent
            );
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}
Sample Input
3
1 CAR101 2000 3
2 BIKE201 500 4
3 TRUCK301 3000 2
Calculation
Car:
2000 × 3 = 6000
Bike:
500 × 4 × 0.90 = 1800
Truck:
3000 × 2 × 1.20 = 7200
Output
CAR101 6000.00
BIKE201 1800.00
TRUCK301 7200.00
Where is Runtime Polymorphism?
This is the important part:
Vehicle vehicle;

vehicle = new Car(...);
vehicle.calculateRent(days);
or
vehicle = new Bike(...);
vehicle.calculateRent(days);
or
vehicle = new Truck(...);
vehicle.calculateRent(days);
The reference is of type Vehicle, but the method that executes depends on the actual object created at runtime.
